 6). Still, an egregiously high price may be held unconscionable by courts if it occurs in\nconnection with unfair surprise or the absence of meaningful choice. A price term itself may\nsurprise consumers, for example, when a product is sold via a complex pricing scheme. A\nconsumer may be unfairly surprised when the price is multidimensional and certain price\ndimensions do not affect the contracting decisions. A consumer may also be unfairly surprised\nwhen the price is contingent or deferred and the consumer underestimates the likelihood of\ntriggering the relevant price or the importance of certain deferred price dimensions (e.g., a long-\nterm interest rate that replaces a low teaser rate after the introductory period; or the long-term,\n119\n@§6\n"